16 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows an experimental timeline for fish involving post-fertilization events, rehousing into size pairs, separation, reunion, hormone collection, and euthanasia. Panel B presents a box plot comparing initiated approaches between larger and smaller fish, with larger fish initiating more approaches; statistical significance is indicated \(p=0.0078\). Panel C displays a box plot comparing the number of displacements between larger and smaller fish, showing larger fish cause more displacements and statistical significance \(p=0.0078\).) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Two scatter plots labeled A and B present correlations between difference in mass and social behavior metrics, each with a red trend line. Plot A shows difference in mass versus difference in initiated approaches, with p-value less than 0.0001. Plot B shows difference in mass versus difference in displacements, with p-value 0.0015. Both plots display positive correlations, with individual data points marked by black dots. Axes are labeled with respective variables and units in grams.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Figure with four panels shows the association between cortisol levels and social behaviors. Panel A: scatterplot with a negative nonlinear trend between initiated approaches and cortisol, with points colored by individual\220s relative size. Panel B: similar plot for displacements, again showing lower cortisol with more displacements. Panel C: boxplot comparing initiated approaches for high versus low cortisol groups, with significantly more approaches in the low cortisol group. Panel D: boxplot showing a trend toward more displacements in the low cortisol group compared to high cortisol, though not statistically significant.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Fluorescent microscopy composite showing five labeled panels: \(A\) Cell-level view with DAPI in blue, HuC/D in green, DCX in red, and insets highlighting co-labeled cells; \(B\) coronal brain section with DAPI and pS6, brain regions outlined; \(C\) coronal brain section with DAPI and DCX, regions outlined; \(D\) \215No Primary Controls\216 with separate DAPI, DCX, and pS6 channels showing normal DAPI staining but reduced DCX/pS6 staining; \(E\) \215No Secondary Controls\216 with the same channel separation and normal DAPI staining but reduced DCX/pS6 staining, all with consistent region labeling and scale bars provided.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Six-panel scientific figure showing scatter plots with trend lines, each labeled A to F. Panels A and B display negative correlations between total pS6+ or DCX+ cell counts and initiated approaches. Panels C and D show negative correlations between these cell counts and displacements. Panels E and F depict positive correlations between cell counts and cortisol levels. All plots include individual data points, fitted trend lines, axis labels, and significance values, with p-values less than 0.0001 or 0.0002.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Composite figure with five panels labeled A to E. Panel A is a bar chart showing the percentage of explained variance by principal component dimensions, with percentages decreasing from 43 percent in dimension one to 0.6 percent in dimension seven. Panel B is a biplot with PC1 and PC2 loadings, showing vectors for variables such as cortisol, ps6, displacement, approaches, and others, color-coded by their contribution. Panel C is a scatterplot of principal components with points colored by group, larger and smaller. Panel D is a boxplot comparing PC1 scores between larger and smaller groups with a p-value of 0.041, and Panel E is a boxplot of PC2 scores by the same groups with a p-value of 0.038.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
